+
+def render(inst: dict) -> str:
+    rules = inst["rules"]
+    lines = [f"  {i}: {json.dumps(children, separators=(',', ':'))}" for i, children in enumerate(rules)]
+    statement = f"""Compute a certified odd-k colour-connection number of a compact tree.
+
+A finite rooted tree is specified by the acyclic grammar below. Type 0 is a
+single leaf. An occurrence of any other type i is one new root joined by an edge
+to the root of a FRESH, DISJOINT copy of every child type listed on line i.
+Repeated child identifiers mean repeated fresh copies; their multiplicity matters.
+The order of children does not matter. The whole tree is type {inst['root']}.
+Expanding the grammar gives exactly {inst['expanded_vertices']} vertices and
+{inst['expanded_edges']} edges; this count is supplied only as a check.
+
+An edge-colouring assigns a positive-integer colour to every edge. Because this
+object is a tree, two vertices have a unique path. The colour-distance of two
+vertices is the number of distinct colours on that path. A colouring is
+k-colour-connecting when every pair has colour-distance at most k. cc_k(T) is
+the maximum number of distinct edge colours in such a colouring. Here k is the
+odd integer {inst['k']}, and r=(k-1)/2={inst['radius']}.
+
+For this rooted tree, the paper's Algorithm 1 starts every vertex with state
+(a,b)=(0,0), processes children before parents, and applies one update per child:
+
+* if the child's b equals r, add its a to the parent's a and set parent b=r;
+* otherwise add child a+1 to parent a and replace parent b by
+  max(parent b, child b+1).
