diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f17c61c93c06bc45b5e373b3dc7e254d6e3da019
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,146 @@
+# Odd-`k` colour connection on compact trees
+
+**Status: locally verified, not yet shippable.** G1–G8 pass, but the mandatory
+four-vendor hardening and G9 arms could not run because the configured OpenRouter
+key returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` on every redraw. The
+error-only transcript is retained; no oracle failures have been inferred from it.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | other (bottom-up tree dynamic programming) |
+| certificate | exact symbolic root state `[cc_k(T), a(root), b(root)]` |
+| intuition | decomposition: quotient identical repeated subtrees by grammar type |
+| domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## Problem and trust basis
+
+The source is Huang and Li, [*Hardness results for three kinds of colored
+connections of graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.01948). A tree is supplied by
+an acyclic grammar: a type is a new root with fresh copies of its listed child
+types. The solver must compute the root state of Algorithm 1 and its output,
+which Theorem 5.2 identifies with the maximum number `cc_k(T)` of edge colours
+whose every tree path uses at most `k` distinct colours. `verify` independently
+recomputes every grammar type with exact integer additions and checks the three
+submitted integers.
+
+This is deliberately Track B. Section 5 states `cc_1(T)=1`; Lemma 5.1 supplies
+the extremal tree-colouring structure; Algorithm 1 handles odd `k`; and Theorem
+5.2 gives an `O(N)` algorithm on the explicit `N`-vertex tree. Lemma 4.4 also
+gives polynomial verification for a fixed `k`-colour-connection colouring on a
+general graph. Thus a Track A claim would be false. Over eight shipping seeds,
+the explicit Algorithm 1 reference averaged **6,092,042 vertices, 11,462,459
+exact additions, and 1.97 s**. Reusing one state per grammar type needs **90
+exact additions**. The generator carries those states forward as each grammar
+rule is composed; it does not solve a finished instance.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(seed=3, n=4, lag_max=2, radius=1)`, `render` gives the
+following complete data and conventions (the prose definition is the same as
+the module's renderer):
+
+```text
+Type 0 is a leaf. An occurrence of type i is a new root joined to fresh,
+disjoint copies of every listed child. Repeats are separate copies and child
+order is irrelevant. The whole tree is type 3, with 9 vertices and 8 edges.
+k=3 and r=(k-1)/2=1.
+
+Algorithm 1 starts every vertex at (a,b)=(0,0). For each child: if child b=r,
+add child a to parent a and set parent b=r; otherwise add child a+1 and set
+parent b=max(parent b,child b+1). Its output is a(root)+1.
+
+0: []
+1: [0]
+2: [1,0]
+3: [2,2]
+
+Return [value,root_a,root_b] as JSON inside <answer></answer> tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>[5,4,1]</answer>`. `verify(inst, [5,4,1])` returns
+`(True, "ok")`; dropping the last entry returns
+`(False, "answer must contain exactly three integers")`. A person can solve
+this demo on paper in four small state updates.
+
+## Difficulty
+
+Sizes below use seed 19. Larger `n` adds grammar types; `escalate` decreases the
+maximum back-reference lag at fixed answer length, increasing the expanded tree.
+
+| preset | types | `k` | expanded vertices | compact additions | ships? |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 3 | 9 | 8 | no; illustration |
+| easy | 40 | 11 | 5,287,923 | 90 | tentative |
+| medium | 42 | 11 | 12,077,397 | 94 | no |
+| hard | 44 | 11 | 33,229,093 | 98 | no |
+
+## Gates
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 planted | 12/12; JSON round-trip 12/12 |
+| G2 corruptions | 5/5 rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 parser | realistic prose/fence/tag response round-trips |
+| G4 guessing | 0/200,000 from the structure-aware language; space 5,287,922 |
+| G5 density/cost | exact density 1/5,287,922; demo enumeration finds one; reference 11,462,459 additions |
+| G6 attacks | five attacks at 0/8; expanded reference and compact route 8/8 |
+| G7 scaling | doubling types gives 33,993,007,218,015 expanded vertices and still verifies |
+| G8 canonical key | 60/60 relabellings invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | size passes (23 worst-case chars, 3 atoms, 90 operations); oracle component **pending** |
+
+The failed attacks were the subtree-edge upper bound, first-child greedy
+evaluation, deduplicating repeated child types, omitting Algorithm 1's cutoff,
+and 256 random restarts. These are recorded separately from the successful
+Track B reference algorithm.
+
+## Oracle and G9 evidence
+
+The only attempted bare run stopped before any scored attempt:
+
+| model redraw | seed | scored? | result |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | 1717094546 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | 1147686145 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| Claude Sonnet 5 | 365466045 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | 1918472906 | no | HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+| G9 arm | solved / attempts | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | not run |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | not run; gated verdict pending |
+| placebo | 0 / 0 | not run |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is therefore not estimable. The structural hint names only
+the invariant that occurrences of one subtree type have equal states; it does
+not give a follow-on procedure. After OpenRouter capacity is restored, all
+three arms must be rerun before this family can ship.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2001_01948 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=19, **DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+print(render(inst))
+assert verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, once G9 has passed:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2001.01948 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This benchmark is not a complexity-theoretic hardness claim. Memoized dynamic
+programming solves the compact input efficiently; the intended difficulty is
+performing the exact quotient calculation without tools. The reported guessing
+probability is uniform over every feasible root `a` with the freely deducible
+`b=r`; it does not model a learned prior over outputs of these grammars. The
+panel does not include a trained predictor for root states or the missing
+four-vendor oracle run. SHA-256 is used only for an isomorphism-invariant key,
+so its ordinary collision assumption is also a canonicalization caveat.
