   283	\section{NP-hardness for the conflict-free connection number}
   284	
   285	Let $C(G)$ be the subgraph of $G$ induced by the set of cut-edges of $G$,
   286	and let $h(G) = max\{cfc(T): \text{ $T$ is a component of $C(G)$}\}$. The following result
   287	was obtained in \cite{CJV}.
   288	
   289	\begin{lem}\cite{CJV}\label{cfcnp}
   290	If $G$ is a connected graph with cut-edges, then
   291	 $$h(G) \leq cfc(G) \leq h(G) + 1.$$ \\
   292	Moreover, the bounds are sharp.
   293	\end{lem}
   294	
   295	In the following, our aim is to reduce the NAE-3SAT problem to the problem of deciding whether $cfc(G)=h(G)$ or $h(G)+1$ for the case when $h(G)=2$.
   296	We then know that deciding whether $cfc(G)=2$ or $3$ is NP-complete by Theorem \ref{cfcthm}. At first, we need the following lemma.
   297	\begin{lem}\label{cfclem}
   298	Let $G$ be a graph such that $G$ contains two edge-disjoint Hamilton paths $P_1$ and $P_2$.
   299	The edges of $P_1$ is colored with 1 and the edges of $P_2$ is colored with 2.
   300	Then between any pair of vertices of $G$, there exists a monochromatic color 1 path,
   301	a monochromatic color 2 path, a conflict-free path with color 1 used only once and a conflict-free path with color 2 used only once.
   302	\end{lem}
   303	\begin{pf}
   304	Let $u$ and $v$ be two vertices of $G$. Clearly, $u$ and $v$ lie on $P_1$, then the path $uP_1v$ is a monochromatic color 1 path.
   305	Similarly, the path $uP_2v$ is a monochromatic color 2 path.
   306	Now consider the path $P_2$. Let $e$ be the edge incident with $u$ on the path $uP_2v$.
   307	Then $P_2-e$ contains two component $P_u$ and $P_v$ ($u$ lies on $P_u$). Since $P_1$ is a Hamilton path,
   308	there exists an edge $xy$ on $P_1$ such that $x\in V(P_u), y\in V(P_v)$. Now we have that the path $uP_2xyP_2v$ is a conflict-free path with color 1 used only once.
   309	Similarly, there exists a conflict-free path with color 2 used only once.
   310	\end{pf}
   311	
   312	Next we need the lemma given by M Ji et al.~\cite{JLZ} as following:
   313	
   314	\begin{lem} \label{jlz} \cite{JLZ}
   315	Given a connected graph $G$ and a coloring $f$ : $E \ (\text{or} \ V )\rightarrow \{1, 2,\cdots,$ $k\}$ $(k \geq 1)$ of
   316	$G$, determining whether or not $G$ is, respectively, conflict-free connected, conflict-free vertex-connected,
   317	strongly conflict-free connected under the coloring $f$ can be done in polynomial-time.
   318	\end{lem}
   319	
   320	Our main result of this section is as follows.
   321	
