   336	\caption{The graph $I_j$ representing for the clause $c_j$ such that the variable $x_i=l_{j,1}$ is positive in $c_j$.}
   337	\label{cfc2}
   338	\end{figure}
   339	
   340	\begin{thm}\label{cfcthm}
   341	Let $G$ be a graph such that $h(G)=2$. Then deciding whether $cfc(G)=2$ or 3 is NP-complete.
   342	In particular, computing $cfc(G)$ is NP-hard.
   343	\end{thm}
   344	
   345	\begin{pf}
   346	As a result of Lemma \ref{jlz}, the problem is in NP. Then we prove that the problem is NP-complete by reducing the NAE-3SAT problem to it.
   347	Given a 3CNF $\phi=\wedge^m_{j=1}c_j$ on variables $x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_n$, the clause $c_j$ is made up of three distinct literals
   348	$l_{j,1}, l_{j,2}, l_{j,3}$ for each $j\in [m]$. We construct a graph $G_\phi$ as follows.
   349	
   350	First, we construct the variable garget $H_i$ for a variable $x_i$ for each $i\in [n]$.
   351	We start from a complete graph on four vertices $\{t_{i,3}, t_{i,4}, t_{i,5}, t_{i,6}\}$.
   352	Next we add two pendent vertices $t_{i,7}, t_{i,8}$ such that each of the vertices $t_{i,7}, t_{i,8}$ is adjacent to $t_{i,6}$.
   353	Then we get $H_i$ by adding a cycle $t_{i,0}t_{i,1}t_{i,3}t_{i,4}t_{i,2}t_{i,0}$ of length 5.
   354	
   355	Second, we construct the graph $I_j$ representing for the clause $c_j$ for each $j\in[m]$.
   356	We start from a complete graph on four vertices $\{u_{j,0}, u_{j,1}, u_{j,2}, u_{j,3}\}$.
   357	Next we add two pendent vertices $u_{j,4}$ and $u_{j,5}$ such that each of $u_{j,4}$ and $u_{j,5}$ is adjacent to $u_{j,3}$.
   358	Let the resulting graph be $I_{j,u}$. Then we add two copies $I_{j,v}$ and $I_{j,w}$ of $I_{j,u}$. Finally,
   359	we get $I_j$ by adding the set of edges $\{u_{j,0}v_{j,0}, v_{j,0}w_{j,0}, w_{j,0}x_{j,0}\}$ to the union of $I_{j,u}$, $I_{j,v}$ and $I_{j,w}$.
   360	If a literal $l_{j,1}=x_i$ is positive in the clause $c_j$ for some $i\in[n]$, then $u_{j,0}$ is adjacent to $t_{i,1}$.
   361	If a literal $l_{j,1}=\overline x_i$ is negative in the clause $c_j$ for some $i\in[n]$,
   362	then $v_{j,0}$ is adjacent to $t_{j,2}$. We do this similarly for the literals $l_{j,2}$ and $l_{j,3}$.
   363	
   364	Third, we add some extra vertices and edges to complete the construction of the graph $G_\phi$.
   365	We construct a complete graph on $n+1$ vertices $\{t_{1,0}, t_{2,0}, \cdots, t_{n,0}, s_0\}$.
   366	Next we add two vertices $s_1$ and $s_2$ such that $s_0s_1s_2$ is a path of length two. Then the resulting graph is $G_\phi$.
   367	
   368	In the following, we only need to show that $cfc(G_\phi)=2$ if and only if $\phi$ is satisfiable.
   369	
   370	Suppose $cfc(G_\phi)=2$. Notice that $u_{j,4}u_{j,3}u_{j,5}$ is the only path between the vertices $u_{j,4}$ and $u_{j,5}$ (see Figure \ref{cfc2}).
   371	Then we can assume that the edge $u_{j,4}u_{j,3}$ is colored with 1 and the edge $u_{j,5}u_{j,3}$ is colored with 2.
   372	Similarly, we can assume that the edges $v_{j,4}v_{j,3}$, $w_{j,4}w_{j,3}$ are colored with 1
   373	and the edges $v_{j,5}v_{j,3}$, $w_{j,5}w_{j,3}$ are colored with 2. We can assume that $s_0s_1$ is colored with 1 and $s_1s_2$ is colored with 2,
   374	since $s_0s_1s_2$ is the only path between vertices $s_0$ and $s_2$ (see Figure \ref{cfc1}).
   375	There exists a monochromatic color 1 path between vertices $s_0$ and $u_{j,3}$, since color 1 has been used at least twice
   376	on the conflict-free path between vertices $s_2$ and $u_{j,4}$. Similarly, there exists a monochromatic color 2 path
   377	between $s_0$ and $u_{j,3}$. The graph $I_j$ has exact three entries, then these three edges can not have a same color;
   378	otherwise, we can not get both a monochromatic color 1 path and a monochromatic color 2 path between $s_0$ and $u_{j,3}$.
   379	
   380	\textbf{Claim.} There exists no monochromatic path going through some $I_j$.

 succeeded in 103ms:
