   562	
   563	Given a connected graph $G$, it is NP-complete to decide if $cc_1(G)$ attains its trivial lower bound $m(G)-n(G)+2$.
   564	
   565	\begin{thm}\label{cck1}
   566	Given a graph $G$, deciding whetehr $cc_1(G)= m(G)-n(G)+2$ or $cc_1(G)> m(G)-n(G)+2$ is NP-complete.
   567	\end{thm}
   568	\begin{pf}
   569	The problem is in NP by Lemma \ref{NP}. We will prove that it is NP-complete by reducing the 3SAT problem to it.
   570	Given a 3CNF $\phi=\wedge^m_{j=1}c_j$ on variables $x_1,x_2,\cdots,x_n$. The variable $x_i$ appears in $m_i$ clauses for each $i\in[n]$.
   571	Notice that $m=\frac{1}{3}\Sigma_{i=1}^n m_i$. Then we construct a graph $G_{\phi}$ on $38m+1$ vertices as follows.
   572	
   573	First, we construct the variable garget $H_i$ for a variable $x_i$, where $H_i$ is obtained from a cycle of order $6m_i$
   574	with vertices $u_{i,1},u_{i,2},\cdots,u_{i,6m_i}$ such that for each $j\in[m_i]$, a new path $u_{i,6j-5}v_{i,2j-1}v_{i,2j}$ of length 2 is added to each vertex of $u_{i,6j}$.
   575	
   576	Second, we construct the graph $I_j$ representing for the clause $c_j$ for $j\in[m]$. We start form a path $s_{j,1}s_{j,2}s_{j,3}s_{j,4}s_{j,5}$ of length 4.
   577	Then we add an edge $s_{j,3}s$ such that $s_{j,3}$ is adjacent to the global vertex $s$ for each $k\in[m]$. If a variable $x_i$ is positive in clause $c_j$,
   578	then we add three new vertices $w_{i,3k-2},w_{i,3k-1},w_{i,3k}$ and a set of edges
   579	$\{w_{i,3k}s_{j,4}, w_{i,3k}w_{i,3k-1}, w_{i,3k}w_{i,3k-2}, w_{i,3k}u_{i,6k-1}, w_{i,3k-1}u_{i,6k-2}, w_{i,3k-2}u_{i,6k-3}\}$ for each $k\in[m_i]$.
   580	If a variable $x_i$ is negative in clause $c_j$, then we add three new vertices $w_{i,3k-2},w_{i,3k-1},w_{i,3k}$ and a set of edges
   581	$\{w_{i,3k}s_{j,4}, w_{i,3k}w_{i,3k-1}, w_{i,3k}w_{i,3k-2},\\ w_{i,3k}u_{i,6k-5}, w_{i,3k-1}u_{i,6k-4}, w_{i,3k-2}u_{i,6k-3}\}$ for each $k\in[m_i]$.
   582	
   583	Third, let $V_0=\{v_{i,2k}, u_{i,6k-2}, s_{j,1}| j\in[m_i], i\in[n], k\in[m_i]\}$, $|V_0|=7m$.
   584	Then we add the edges from an edge set $\{st | s,t\in V_0\}$. After deleting the edges of a Hamilton path $P$ on the vertex set $V_0$,
   585	we get the graph $\overline {G_\phi}$. Now $G_\phi$ is defined.
   586	
   587	In the following, we only need to show that $cc_1(G_{\phi})>m(G_\phi)-38m+1$ if and only if $\phi$ is satisfiable.
   588	
   589	Suppose that $\phi$ is satisfiable. Then we define a 1-color-connection coloring $f$ on $G\phi$ using $m(G_\phi)-38l+2$ colors.
   590	First, the edges on the path $P$ receive a same color, then the color tree $P$ wastes $7l-2$ colors.
   591	Next, suppose $x_i=0$. Then we give a clockwise coloring such that path $u_{i,6k-5}v_{i,2k}u_{i,6k-4}v_{i,2k-1}$ is a color tree.
   592	If a variable $x_i$ is positive in clause $c_j$ for some $j\in[m]$, then the path $u_{i,6k-3}w_{i,3k-1}u_{i,6k-4}u_{i,6k-2}$ is a color tree,
   593	the path $w_{i,3k}u_{i,6k-1}w_{i,3k-1}w_{i,3k-2}$ is a color tree, and the path $u_{i,6k-1}u_{i,6k+1}u_{i,6k-2}u_{i,6k}$ is a color tree for some $k\in[m_i]$.
   594	If a variable $x_i$ is negative in clause $c_j$ for some $j\in[m]$, then the path $w_{i,3k}u_{i,6k-2}u_{i,6k-4}w_{i,3k-2}$ $w_{i,3k-1}u_{i,6k-3}$ is a color tree,
   595	and the path $u_{i,6k-1}u_{i,6k+1}u_{i,6k-2}u_{i,6k}$ is a color tree. Suppose $x_i=1$. Then we give an anticlockwise coloring
   596	such that $u_{i,6k}v_{i,2k}u_{i,6k+1}v_{i,2k-1}$ is a color tree ($u_{i,6m_i+1}=u_{i,1}$). If a variable $x_i$ is positive in clause $c_j$ for some $j\in[m]$,
   597	then the path $w_{i,3k}u_{i,6k-2}u_{i,6k}w_{i,3k-2}w_{i,3k-1}u_{i,6k-1}$ is a color tree, and the path $u_{i,6k-3}u_{i,6k-5}u_{i,6k-2}u_{i,6k-4}$ is a color tree.
   598	If a variable $x_i$ is negative in clause $c_j$ for some $j\in[m]$, then the path $u_{i,6k-1}w_{i,3k-1}u_{i,6k}u_{i,6k-2}$ is a color tree,
   599	the path $w_{i,3k}u_{i,6k-3}w_{i,3k-1}w_{i,3k-2}$ is a color tree, and the path $u_{i,6k-3}u_{i,6k-5}u_{i,6k-2}u_{i,6k-4}$ is a color tree.
   600	Without loss of generality, we can assume that the value of the first literal corresponding to the variable $x_i$ is true in clause $c_j$, see Figure \ref{mc4}.
   601	The path $s_{j,2}ss_{j_1}s_{j,3}$ is a color tree. Notice that the edge $u_{i,6k-4}w_{i,3k}$ has been connected by some color tree.
   602	Then we can construct a color tree on four vertices and a color tree on five vertices to connect the remaining edges in $I_j$ for each $j\in[m]$.
   603	Now, all the nontrivial color trees are defined, and these color trees connect every edges in $\overline {G_\phi}$.
   604	Then $f$ is a 1-color connection coloring of $G_\phi$. We have that $W_1(G)\leq7m-2+\sum_{i=1}^n 8m_i+\sum_{j=1}^m(3+2+2)=38m-2$.
   605	Clearly, $cc_1(G_{\phi})>m(G_\phi)-38m+1$.
   606	
   607	Suppose that $cc_1(G_{\phi})>m(G_\phi)-38m+1$. Let $f$ be an extremal 1-color connection coloring of $G_{\phi}$.
   608	Then $f$ consists of several nontrivial color trees that connect all the edges in $\overline G_{\phi}$.
   609	Clearly, the vertices of $V_0$ are in a same color tree; otherwise, we have to waste extra colors to connect the edges in $\{st: s,t\in V_0\}$.
   610	Let $T$ be the color tree that contains the vertices of $V_0$. Suppose that the vertex $s_{j,4}$ is in $T$ for some $j\in[m]$.
   611	Let $x_i$ be a variable corresponding to the literal $l_{j,1}$ in the clause $c_j$.
   612	Then the vertex $w_{i,3k}$ is also in $T$ for some $k\in[m_i]$. Similar thing happens for the literals $l_{j,2}$ and $l_{j,3}$.
   613	We have that $W_1(G)\geq 7l-2+\sum_{i=1}^n 9m_i+\sum_{j=1}^m(1+1+2)+1=38m-1$, a contradiction.
   614	As a result, the vertex $s_{j,4}$ is not in $T$, and the color tree $T$ is exact $P$.
   615	There are more than 5 wasted edges related to $s_{j,4}$ by Lemma \ref{p}.
   616	We have that $W_1(G)\geq 7l-2+\sum_{i=1}^n 8m_i+\sum_{j=1}^m(5+2)=38m-2$.
   617	Next we only need to consider the situation when equality holds since $cc_1(G_{\phi})>m(G_\phi)-38m+1$.
   618	Then the variable garget $H_i$ has a ``clockwise" or ``anticlockwise" coloring consisting of some color trees $P_4$ or $P_6$ in Figure \ref{mc23};
   619	otherwise, $f$ is not an extremal 1-color connection coloring. If $f$ is a clockwise coloring restricted to $H_i$ such that
   620	the path $u_{i,6k-5}v_{i,2k}u_{i,6k-4}v_{i,2k-1}$ is a color tree, then $x_i=0$. If $f$ is an anticlockwise coloring restricted to $H_i$
   621	such that the path $u_{i,6k-5}v_{i,2k}u_{i,6k-4}v_{i,2k-1}$ is a color tree, then $x_i=1$. Since the equality holds, there are only 5 wasted edges left
   622	for the remaining edges in $I_j$ for each $j\in[m]$. Then there exists at least one literal in $c_j$ with true value for each $j\in[m]$.
   623	Thus, $f$ is a 1-color connection coloring such that $\phi$ is satisfiable.
   624	
   625	Finally, we have that $cc_1(G_{\phi})>m(G_\phi)-38m+1$ if and only if $\phi$ is satisfiable. The proof is now complete. \qed
   626	
   627	\end{pf}
   628	
   629	 Therefore, we have the following corollary.
   630	
   631	\begin{cor}
   632	Givin a graph $G$, it is NP-hard to compute the monochromatic connection number $mc(G)$ of $G$.
   633	\end{cor}
   634	
   635	
   636	\section{A good algorithm for the $k$-color connection number of trees}
   637	
   638	Although to compute the $k$-color connection number for a general graph is NP-hard even for $k=1$,
   639	it is polynomial-time to compute the number for some special graphs.
   640	Let $T$ be a tree with $n$ vertices. Clearly, $cc_1(T)=1$. Next, we are going to find
   641	an extremal $k$-color connecting coloring for trees with some nice properties, which will be used
   642	for our algorithms.
   643	
   644	\begin{lem}\label{treealg}
   645	For a tree $T$, there exist an extremal $k$-color connection coloring $f$ of $T$ satisfying the following properties.
   646	\begin{enumerate}
   647	\item Each color forest $F_c$ is a color tree $T_c$.
   648	\item If $T_c$ and $T_{c^\star}$ are two nontrivial color trees, then $T_c$ and $T_{c^\star}$ share a common vertex.
   649	\item If $T_c$ is a nontrivial color tree and $v$ is a pendent vertex, then $cd\big(\{v\}, V(T_c)\big)\leq \lfloor\frac{k}{2}\rfloor$.
   650	\end{enumerate}
   651	\end{lem}
   652	
   653	\begin{pf}
   654	 Assume to the contrary, there exist a color forest $F_c$ containing two or more components.
   655	 We do some contraction operations on $T$. First, we contract the edge $e\in E(T)$ if $E(F_c)$ lie in one component of $T\backslash e$.
   656	 Next we contract all the edges of $F_c$. Finally, we pick a pendent vertex $v_0$ of the resulting graph,
   657	 and let $e_0$ be the edge adjacent to $v_0$. Clearly, $v_0$ represents for a component $C_0$ of $F_c$.
   658	 We recolor the edges of $C_0$ with color $f(e_0)$. Let $P=s\cdots t$ be the path between vertices $s$ and $t$.
   659	 Then the new coloring $f^\star$ is still an extremal $k$-color connection coloring. However, the number of
   660	 total number of components of $F_c$ decreases by one, a contradiction, and the first property is thus proved.
   661	
   662	 For the second property, assume to the contrary, we have $cd\big(V(T_c),V(T_{c^\star})\big)\geq1$
   663	 and the shortest path that uses minimum number of colors is $aa_0\cdots b$. Let $H$ be an induced subgraph by
   664	 deleting the edges of the path $aa_0\cdots b$ from $E(T)$, and the component which contains $T_c$ is $A$,
   665	 as well as the component which contains $T_{c^\star}$ is $B$. Without loss of generality,
   666	 assume that for a pendant vertex $v\in V(A)$ we have $cd(v,a)\leq \lfloor \frac{k}{2}\rfloor$.
   667	 Then we recolor an edge adjacent to $a$ in $T_c$ with color $f(aa_0)$. The new coloring $f^\star$ is still an
   668	 extremal $k$-color connecting coloring. However, the number of edges of $T_c$ decreases by one, a contradiction.
   669	
   670	 For the third property, again assume to the contrary, there exists a pendent vertex $v_0$ such that
   671	 $cd\big(\{v_0\}, V(T_c)\big)\geq \lfloor\frac{k}{2}\rfloor+1$. Let $v_0\cdots t_0t$ be the path that uses $cd\big(\{v_0\}, V(T_c)\big)$ colors,
   672	 and $v$ be a pendent vertex such that the path $v\cdots t_1t_2\cdots t$ goes through a pendent vertex $t_1$ of $T_c$.
   673	 Then we exchange the colors of the edges $t_0t$ and $t_1t_2$. The new coloring $f^\star$ is still an extremal
   674	 $k$-color connecting coloring. However, the length of the path that uses $cd\big(\{v_0\}, V(T_c)\big)$ colors decreases by one, a contradiction. \qed
   675	
   676	\end{pf}
   677	
   678	\begin{algorithm}
   679	\caption{$cc_k(T)$ for $k$ is odd}
   680	\label{odd}
   681	
   682	\KwIn {A connected rooted tree $T$ with $V(T)=\{v_1, v_2, \cdots, v_n\}$; the root vertex $v_n$ lies on a path with length the diameter of
   683	$T$ such that the distance between each end point of the path and $v_n$ is at least $\lfloor\frac{k}{2}\rfloor$; if $j > i$,
   684	then $v_j$ is the parent of $v_i \in V(T)$; an odd integer $k$}
   685	\KwOut { $a(v_n)+1$ }
   686	\For{$i=1; i\leq n$}
   687	    {
   688	       $a(v_i)=0;
   689	       b(v_i)=0$\;
   690	    }
   691	  \For{$i=1; i\leq n$}
   692	    {
   693	       \While{$v_j$ is the parent of $v_i$}
   694	         {
   695	           \eIf{$b(v_i)=\frac{k-1}{2}$}
   696	           {
   697	           $a(v_j)=a(v_i)+a(v_j)$\;
   698	           $b(v_j)=b(v_i)$\;
   699	           }
   700	           {
   701	           $a(v_j)=a(v_i)+a(v_j)+1$\;
   702	           $b(v_j)=max\{b(v_j),b(v_i)+1\}$\;
   703	           }
   704	         }
   705	    }
   706	    return $a(v_n)+1$\;
   707	\end{algorithm}
   708	
   709	\begin{algorithm}[h]
   710	\caption{$cc_k(T)$ for $k$ is even}
   711	\label{even}
   712	
   713	\KwIn {A connected rooted tree $T$ with $V(T)=\{v_1, v_2, \cdots, v_n\}$; the root vertex $v_n$ lies on the path with length the
   714	diameter of $T$ such that the distance between each end point of the path and $v_n$ is at least $\lfloor\frac{k}{2}\rfloor$;
   715	if $j > i$, then $v_j$ is the parent of $v_i \in V(T)$; an even integer $k$}
   716	\KwOut { $a(v_n)+\Delta$}
   717	\For{$i=1; i\leq n$}
   718	    {
   719	       $a(v_i)=0;
   720	       b(v_i)=0$\;
   721	    }
   722	$S=\emptyset$\;
   723	  \For{$i=1; i\leq n$}
   724	    {
   725	       \While{$v_j$ is the parent of $v_i$}
   726	         {
   727	           \eIf{$b(v_i)=\frac{k}{2}-1$}
   728	           {
   729	           $a(v_j)=a(v_i)+a(v_j)$\;
   730	           $b(v_j)=b(v_i)$\;
   731	           $S=S\cup \{v_iv_j\}$\;
   732	           }
   733	           {
   734	           $a(v_j)=a(v_i)+a(v_j)+1$\;
   735	           $b(v_j)=max\{b(v_j),b(v_i)+1\}$\;
   736	           }
   737	         }
   738	    }
   739	  $\Delta$ is the maximum degree of the subgraph induced by edge set $S$\;
   740	  return $a(v_n)+\Delta$\;
   741	\end{algorithm}
   742	
   743	\begin{thm}\label{linear}
   744	For a tree $T$ on $n$ vertices, Algorithms \ref{odd} and \ref{even} can compute $cc_k(T)$ in a linear time
   745	of $n$.
   746	\end{thm}
   747	
   748	\begin{pf}
   749	Let the root $u$ be one of the middle vertices (or vertex) on the path with length the diameter of the tree $T$.
   750	We then use Breadth-First-Search algorithm to get a rooted tree $T$ with Property 1.
   751	When $k$ is odd, the ``for" loops in Algorithm \ref{odd} computes the maximum number of distinct colors
   752	outside the unique nontrivial color tree. Hence, Algorithm \ref{odd} computes $cc_k(T)$ by Lemma \ref{treealg}.
   753	When $k$ is even, the ``for" loops in Algorithm \ref{odd} computes the maximum number of distinct colors outside
   754	several combined nontrivial color trees. Hence, Algorithm \ref{even} computes $cc_k(T)$ by Lemma \ref{treealg}.
   755	The running time of each iteration of the ``for" loops of Algorithm \ref{odd} or \ref{even} is $\mathcal{O}(1)$.
   756	and so the running time is in linear of $n$. This completes the proof. \qed
   757	\end{pf}
